#, no-wrap
msgid "On the curve you measured, $P_1$ first reaches its maximum at the $\\pi$ pulse ($\\theta = \\pi$). Now suppose a device is miscalibrated, so that a *nominal* angle $\\theta$ on the x-axis actually produces a rotation of $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"
msgstr "Sa curve na nasukat mo, unang naaabot ng $P_1$ ang maximum nito sa $\\pi$ pulse ($\\theta = \\pi$). Ngayon, ipagpalagay na mali ang calibration ng isang device, kaya ang *nominal* na anggulong $\\theta$ sa x-axis ay talagang nagbubunga ng rotation na $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(a)** At what nominal angle would the first maximum of $P_1$ now appear?\n"
"**(b)** Would applying a single $\\pi$ pulse be a sensitive way to detect this 5% error?\n"
msgstr ""
"**(a)** Sa anong nominal na anggulo lalabas ngayon ang unang maximum ng $P_1$?\n"
"**(b)** Magiging sensitibong paraan ba ang paglapat ng isang $\\pi$ pulse para matukoy ang 5% na error na ito?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Sagot\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(a)** The maximum appears where the *actual* rotation equals $\\pi$, that is, at the nominal angle $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; a small leftward shift of the peak.\n"
msgstr "**(a)** Lumalabas ang maximum kung saan ang *aktwal* na rotation ay katumbas ng $\\pi$, ibig sabihin, sa nominal na anggulong $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; bahagyang paglipat ng peak pakaliwa.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(b)** No. A nominal $\\pi$ pulse produces an actual rotation of $1.05\\pi$, giving $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ instead of $1$ &mdash; a change too small to see against measurement noise. This is why, to *calibrate* precisely, NMR spectroscopists amplify the error by applying the rotation many times (for example the $360^\\circ$ method, or a long pulse train), so a small per-pulse error accumulates into a large, measurable deviation. We return to this idea in the challenge problems.\n"
msgstr "**(b)** Hindi. Ang nominal na $\\pi$ pulse ay nagbubunga ng aktwal na rotation na $1.05\\pi$, kaya $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ sa halip na $1$ &mdash; pagbabagong masyadong maliit para makita laban sa measurement noise. Kaya para *mag-calibrate* nang tumpak, pinalalaki ng mga NMR spectroscopist ang error sa pamamagitan ng paglapat ng rotation nang maraming beses (halimbawa ang $360^\\circ$ method, o mahabang pulse train), para ang maliit na error kada pulse ay mag-ipon at maging malaki at masusukat na paglihis. Babalikan natin ang ideyang ito sa mga challenge problem.\n"

