# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Experiment: $T_2$, $T_2^*$ {#experiment-t-2-t-2}"
msgstr "Eksperimento: $T_2$, $T_2^*$ {#experiment-t-2-t-2}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Theory {#theory-3}"
msgstr "Teorya {#theory-3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$T_1$ measures *energy* loss. $T_2$ measures loss of **phase coherence** in the transverse plane. We can observe this with a **Ramsey experiment**: a $\\pi/2$ pulse rotates all the spins down to the equator, all pointing in the same direction. They Larmor precess, but each one precesses at a slightly different frequency, so they fan out along the equator and the net transverse signal decays, with a characteristic decay time of $T_2^*$.\n"
msgstr "Sinusukat ng $T_1$ ang pagkawala ng *enerhiya*. Sinusukat naman ng $T_2$ ang pagkawala ng **phase coherence** sa transverse plane. Maoobserbahan natin ito sa pamamagitan ng **Ramsey experiment**: ibinababa ng isang $\\pi/2$ pulse ang lahat ng spin sa equator, na lahat ay nakaturo sa iisang direksyon. Nag-Larmor precess sila, pero bawat isa ay nagpe-precess sa bahagyang magkaibang frequency, kaya kumakalat sila sa kahabaan ng equator at nabubulok ang kabuuang transverse signal, na may katangiang decay time na $T_2^*$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Aside for quantum readers:** The fanning-out picture shows one way to lose coherence: a spread of definite phases that average out. It's worth knowing this isn't the only mechanism. Coherence can also be lost because the phase becomes entangled with the environment, so it's no longer a definite value the system holds at all. You can't tell the two mechanisms apart just by watching the signal decay, so the fanning-out picture is a good intuition. Be mindful not to read it as the complete or literal story.\n"
msgstr "**Paalala para sa mga quantum na mambabasa:** Ipinapakita ng larawan ng pagkalat ang isang paraan ng pagkawala ng coherence: isang hanay ng mga tiyak na phase na nagko-cancel sa average. Mahalagang malaman na hindi ito ang tanging mekanismo. Maaari ring mawala ang coherence dahil ang phase ay nagiging entangled sa kapaligiran, kaya hindi na ito tiyak na halagang hawak ng sistema. Hindi mo matutukoy ang pagkakaiba ng dalawang mekanismo sa pagmamasid lang sa decay ng signal, kaya magandang intuwisyon ang larawan ng pagkalat. Mag-ingat lang na huwag itong basahin bilang kumpleto o literal na kuwento.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two effects dephase a spin:\n"
"* Irreversible (or effectively irreversible) decoherence arising from uncontrolled coupling to other degrees of freedom, characterized by the true $T_2$. In NMR, this generally comes from molecular motion modulating the couplings between spins. In quantum computing, it corresponds to unwanted couplings between qubits and the environment.\n"
"* Static or slowly-varying, reversible frequency offsets, such as field inhomogeneity, qubit-to-qubit frequency variation, and calibration errors. Combined with the true $T_2$, these produce the shorter apparent decay time $T_2^*$ observed in a Ramsey experiment.\n"
msgstr ""
"Dalawang epekto ang nagdudulot ng dephasing sa isang spin:\n"
"* Irreversible (o halos irreversible) na decoherence na nagmumula sa hindi kontroladong coupling sa ibang degrees of freedom, na inilalarawan ng tunay na $T_2$. Sa NMR, karaniwang galing ito sa galaw ng molecule na nagmo-modulate sa mga coupling sa pagitan ng mga spin. Sa quantum computing, tumutugma ito sa mga hindi ninanais na coupling sa pagitan ng mga qubit at ng kapaligiran.\n"
"* Static o mabagal magbago, reversible na mga frequency offset, tulad ng field inhomogeneity, pagkakaiba ng frequency sa bawat qubit, at mga calibration error. Kapag pinagsama sa tunay na $T_2$, nagbubunga ang mga ito ng mas maikling apparent decay time na $T_2^*$ na nakikita sa Ramsey experiment.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So how do we recover the true $T_2$ if a Ramsey experiment only tells us about $T_2^*$? The second experiment, called a **Hahn echo**, cancels all the noise of the second type to find the true $T_2$. It starts the same as a Ramsey experiment, with a $\\pi/2$ pulse, then the spins fan out as before. After some time $\\tau$, a $\\pi$ pulse is applied. The pulse reverses the phase accumulation caused by static frequency offsets, so the spins refocus after a second delay of the same duration, $\\tau$.\n"
msgstr "Kaya paano natin makukuha ang tunay na $T_2$ kung $T_2^*$ lang ang sinasabi ng Ramsey experiment? Ang pangalawang eksperimento, na tinatawag na **Hahn echo**, ay nagka-cancel ng lahat ng noise ng pangalawang uri para makuha ang tunay na $T_2$. Pareho ang simula nito sa Ramsey experiment, na may $\\pi/2$ pulse, tapos kumakalat ang mga spin gaya ng dati. Pagkalipas ng ilang oras na $\\tau$, may ilalapat na $\\pi$ pulse. Binabaligtad ng pulse ang naipong phase na dulot ng mga static na frequency offset, kaya muling nagre-refocus ang mga spin pagkatapos ng pangalawang delay na kasing-haba ng una, $\\tau$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$T_2^*$ and $T_2$ are both important numbers to know in both the chemistry setting and the quantum computing setting. In the chemistry setting, $T_2^*$ sets your linewidth for a standard NMR experiment (you'll see what this means in a later [section](#nmr-spectroscopy)). $T_2$ can tell you much about the molecules, such as molecular tumbling, and a change in $T_2$ can signal that a molecule has bound to something larger. In the quantum computing setting, we generally want both $T_2$ and $T_2^*$ to be as long as possible, so we have more time to perform experiments before decoherence overwhelms the system. A whole family of techniques based on the Hahn echo extends this time by canceling the reversible noise, so that the circuit is ideally limited by the true $T_2$, not $T_2^*$. This is called \"dynamical decoupling.\"\n"
msgstr "Parehong mahalagang malaman ang $T_2^*$ at $T_2$ sa chemistry at sa quantum computing. Sa chemistry, itinatakda ng $T_2^*$ ang linewidth mo sa karaniwang NMR experiment (makikita mo kung ano ang ibig sabihin nito sa susunod na [seksyon](#nmr-spectroscopy)). Maraming masasabi ang $T_2$ tungkol sa mga molecule, tulad ng molecular tumbling, at ang pagbabago ng $T_2$ ay maaaring magsenyas na may kumapit na mas malaki sa molecule. Sa quantum computing, karaniwang gusto nating mahaba hangga't maaari ang parehong $T_2$ at $T_2^*$, para mas marami tayong oras na magsagawa ng mga eksperimento bago madaig ng decoherence ang sistema. Isang buong pamilya ng mga teknik batay sa Hahn echo ang nagpapahaba ng oras na ito sa pamamagitan ng pag-cancel ng reversible na noise, kaya ang circuit ay ideyal na nililimitahan na lang ng tunay na $T_2$, hindi ng $T_2^*$. Tinatawag itong \"dynamical decoupling.\"\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Implementation {#implementation}"
msgstr "Implementasyon {#implementation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "When we implement the Ramsey and Hahn echo experiments on a qubit, the readout is slightly different than for the NMR case. In NMR, you can directly observe the oscillating fields induced by the spins precessing at the Larmor frequency in the transverse plane. But with a qubit, we can only measure its projection along the $Z$ axis, that is, whether it's $\\vert 0 \\rangle$ or $\\vert 1 \\rangle$. Therefore, for both experiments, we end with an additional $\\pi/2$ pulse followed by a measurement. This $\\pi/2$ pulse translates the phase information of the qubit into a population.\n"
msgstr "Kapag ini-implement natin ang mga Ramsey at Hahn echo experiment sa isang qubit, bahagyang naiiba ang readout kumpara sa NMR. Sa NMR, direkta mong makikita ang mga oscillating field na nililikha ng mga spin na nagpe-precess sa Larmor frequency sa transverse plane. Pero sa qubit, ang masusukat lang natin ay ang projection nito sa $Z$ axis, ibig sabihin, kung $\\vert 0 \\rangle$ o $\\vert 1 \\rangle$ ito. Kaya, para sa parehong eksperimento, tinatapos natin ito sa dagdag na $\\pi/2$ pulse na sinusundan ng measurement. Isinasalin ng $\\pi/2$ pulse na ito ang impormasyon ng phase ng qubit tungo sa populasyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Below we run each experiment to determine $T_2^*$ and $T_2$.\n"
msgstr "Sa ibaba, pinapatakbo natin ang bawat eksperimento para matukoy ang $T_2^*$ at $T_2$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ramsey ($T_2^{*}$): Two $\\pi/2$ pulses with delay time $\\tau$ {#ramsey-t-2-two-pi2-pulses-with-delay-time-tau}"
msgstr "Ramsey ($T_2^{*}$): Dalawang $\\pi/2$ pulse na may delay time na $\\tau$ {#ramsey-t-2-two-pi2-pulses-with-delay-time-tau}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The first $\\pi/2$ pulse tips the Bloch vector to the equator, and the next one translates the phase of the Bloch vector to a population. Because the phase changes in time as the Bloch vector precesses, we see a sinusoidal pattern in the population as a function of precession time $\\tau$. As the qubit loses phase coherence we should see the amplitude of this sine curve decay.\n"
msgstr "Ibinabagsak ng unang $\\pi/2$ pulse ang Bloch vector sa equator, at isinasalin naman ng sumunod ang phase ng Bloch vector tungo sa populasyon. Dahil nagbabago ang phase sa paglipas ng oras habang nagpe-precess ang Bloch vector, nakakakita tayo ng sinusoidal na pattern sa populasyon bilang function ng precession time na $\\tau$. Habang nawawalan ng phase coherence ang qubit, dapat nating makitang nabubulok ang amplitude ng sine curve na ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Some more subtleties for the quantum readers:** In quantum computing experiments, the oscillations we see aren't actually at the Larmor frequency, which is in the GHz range and much too fast for us to resolve. Quantum experiments operate in the *rotating frame*: a frame that spins along with the qubit at (near) the Larmor frequency, so the fast precession is invisible and the Bloch vector looks nearly still. To see fringes, we deliberately set our rotating frame slightly detuned from the Larmor frequency. The Bloch vector then appears to precess at that small detuning, and we get an oscillating signal at the detuning frequency. Below, we don't fix the detuning at one value; we choose it so that about a dozen fringes span the measurement window (which we size to the qubit's coherence time), which keeps the oscillation easy to sample without aliasing. One more wrinkle: in Qiskit we don't have the pulse-level control needed to change the pulse frequency (which is what sets the rotating-frame frequency). Instead, we add an *effective* detuning by hand, using it to set the phase of the second Ramsey pulse. It's a bit contrived, but the result is the same and the decay we observe is still the genuine $T_2^*$.\n"
msgstr "**Ilan pang detalye para sa mga quantum na mambabasa:** Sa mga eksperimento sa quantum computing, ang mga oscillation na nakikita natin ay hindi talaga nasa Larmor frequency, na nasa GHz range at masyadong mabilis para ma-resolve natin. Gumagana ang mga quantum experiment sa *rotating frame*: isang frame na umiikot kasabay ng qubit sa (halos) Larmor frequency, kaya hindi nakikita ang mabilis na precession at halos nakatigil ang tingin sa Bloch vector. Para makakita ng mga fringe, sadyang itinatakda natin ang rotating frame na bahagyang detuned mula sa Larmor frequency. Lumilitaw na nagpe-precess ang Bloch vector sa maliit na detuning na iyon, at nakakakuha tayo ng oscillating signal sa detuning frequency. Sa ibaba, hindi natin itinatakda ang detuning sa iisang halaga; pinipili natin ito para ang mga isang dosenang fringe ay sumaklaw sa measurement window (na sinusukat natin ayon sa coherence time ng qubit), na nagpapanatiling madaling i-sample ang oscillation nang walang aliasing. Isa pang kakaiba: sa Qiskit, wala tayong pulse-level na kontrol na kailangan para baguhin ang pulse frequency (na siyang nagtatakda ng frequency ng rotating frame). Sa halip, nagdaragdag tayo ng *epektibong* detuning nang mano-mano, gamit ito para itakda ang phase ng pangalawang Ramsey pulse. Medyo pilit ito, pero pareho ang resulta at ang decay na nakikita natin ay tunay pa ring $T_2^*$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Bloch-sphere view {#a-bloch-sphere-view-2}"
msgstr "Tanaw sa Bloch sphere {#a-bloch-sphere-view-2}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A $\\pi/2$ pulse tips the spins to the equator; each then precesses at a slightly different rate and they **fan out**, so the net (red) vector shrinks as they dephase — this decay is $T_2^*$.\n"
msgstr "Ibinabagsak ng $\\pi/2$ pulse ang mga spin sa equator; bawat isa ay nagpe-precess sa bahagyang magkaibang bilis at **kumakalat** sila, kaya lumiliit ang kabuuang (pulang) vector habang nagde-dephase sila — ang decay na ito ay $T_2^*$.\n"

