# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "NMR spectroscopy of a molecule: Chemical shift and $J$-coupling {#nmr-spectroscopy-of-a-molecule-chemical-shift-and-j-coupling}"
msgstr "NMR spectroscopy ng isang molecule: Chemical shift at $J$-coupling {#nmr-spectroscopy-of-a-molecule-chemical-shift-and-j-coupling}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "From one spin to a molecule {#from-one-spin-to-a-molecule}"
msgstr "Mula sa isang spin tungo sa isang molecule {#from-one-spin-to-a-molecule}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The experiments so far characterized a single, isolated spin. Real chemical NMR reads a *molecule* (many coupled spins at once) and uses the resulting spectrum to infer features of the molecule's structure. This can tell us about two effects:\n"
msgstr "Ang mga eksperimento hanggang ngayon ay nagkarakterisa ng iisang nakahiwalay na spin. Ang tunay na chemical NMR ay bumabasa ng isang *molecule* (maraming magkakakabit na spin nang sabay-sabay) at ginagamit ang nagreresultang spectrum para ipahiwatig ang mga katangian ng istruktura ng molecule. Makapagsasabi ito sa atin tungkol sa dalawang epekto:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Chemical shift.** Each chemically distinct nucleus precesses at a slightly different frequency $\\nu_i$, because its local electrons shield the applied field by different amounts. Peak *positions* therefore identify chemical environments (a CH$_3$ proton versus an aromatic one, for example).\n"
msgstr "**Chemical shift.** Bawat chemically distinct na nucleus ay nagpe-precess sa bahagyang magkaibang frequency na $\\nu_i$, dahil ang mga lokal nitong electron ay nagsisilbing panangga sa inilapat na field sa magkakaibang antas. Kaya ang mga *posisyon* ng peak ay nagtutukoy ng mga chemical environment (halimbawa, isang CH$_3$ na proton laban sa isang aromatic).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Scalar $J$-coupling.** A through-bond interaction between two spins splits each peak into a multiplet. A nucleus with $n$ *equivalent* neighbors is split into $n+1$ lines with binomial intensities, the **$n+1$ rule**. The splitting *pattern* reveals connectivity (which nuclei are bonded), and the spacing is the coupling constant $J$.\n"
msgstr "**Scalar $J$-coupling.** Isang through-bond na interaksyon sa pagitan ng dalawang spin na naghahati sa bawat peak tungo sa multiplet. Ang nucleus na may $n$ *equivalent* na kapitbahay ay nahahati sa $n+1$ na linya na may binomial na intensity, ang **$n+1$ rule**. Ang *pattern* ng paghahati ay nagpapakita ng connectivity (aling mga nucleus ang magkabigkis), at ang pagitan ay ang coupling constant na $J$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The experiment {#the-experiment}"
msgstr "Ang eksperimento {#the-experiment}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To model this in our quantum computer, we need to implement a spin Hamiltonian on the qubits. In the weak-coupling, high-field limit (chemical-shift differences $\\gg$ couplings), the $XX$ and $YY$ parts of the coupling average out, leaving only $ZZ$. So the Hamiltonian is (angular-frequency units, $\\hbar=1$):\n"
msgstr "Para imodelo ito sa quantum computer natin, kailangan nating mag-implement ng spin Hamiltonian sa mga qubit. Sa weak-coupling, high-field na limit (mga pagkakaiba ng chemical shift $\\gg$ mga coupling), nagka-cancel sa average ang mga bahaging $XX$ at $YY$ ng coupling, at $ZZ$ na lang ang natitira. Kaya ang Hamiltonian ay (sa mga unit ng angular frequency, $\\hbar=1$):\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$H = \\underbrace{\\sum_i \\pi\\,\\nu_i\\, Z_i}_{\\text{chemical shift}} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\sum_{i<j} \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\,J_{ij}\\, Z_iZ_j}_{J\\text{-coupling}}.$$\n"
msgstr "$$H = \\underbrace{\\sum_i \\pi\\,\\nu_i\\, Z_i}_{\\text{chemical shift}} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\sum_{i<j} \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}\\,J_{ij}\\, Z_iZ_j}_{J\\text{-coupling}}.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each nucleus is **one qubit**: the chemical shift is a single-qubit $Z$ rotation, and the coupling is a two-qubit $ZZ$ interaction.\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat nucleus ay **isang qubit**: ang chemical shift ay isang single-qubit na $Z$ rotation, at ang coupling ay isang two-qubit na $ZZ$ na interaksyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The experiment proceeds as follows. A $\\pi/2$ pulse tips every spin into the transverse plane, and the spins precess and couple for time $t$. We record the complex transverse magnetization $s(t) = \\langle X\\rangle + i\\langle Y\\rangle$ summed over all spins. As in the Ramsey experiment above, this transverse magnetization traces out a decaying sinusoid as a function of the precession time $t$. When we take the Fourier transform of the signal, we get a spectrum with peaks at the chemical shift $\\nu_i$ split by the scalar coupling $J_{ij}$.\n"
msgstr "Ganito ang takbo ng eksperimento. Ibinabagsak ng $\\pi/2$ pulse ang bawat spin sa transverse plane, at ang mga spin ay nagpe-precess at nagko-couple sa loob ng oras na $t$. Itinatala natin ang complex transverse magnetization na $s(t) = \\langle X\\rangle + i\\langle Y\\rangle$ na ibinuo mula sa lahat ng spin. Gaya sa Ramsey experiment sa itaas, ang transverse magnetization na ito ay bumubuo ng nabubulok na sinusoid bilang function ng precession time na $t$. Kapag kinuha natin ang Fourier transform ng signal, nakakakuha tayo ng spectrum na may mga peak sa chemical shift na $\\nu_i$ na hinati ng scalar coupling na $J_{ij}$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"We simulate the molecule's evolution by mapping the time $t$ onto *rotation angles*: a larger $t$ means larger $Z$ and $ZZ$ rotations, not a longer or deeper circuit. So while the simulated state does advance with $t$ (that's what makes the free induction decay (FID) oscillate), the physical qubits never idle any longer and there's no $t$-dependent decoherence. The simulated FID therefore never decays on its own. For an infinitely long ideal FID the spectral lines would be infinitely sharp; our finite sampling window instead gives them finite resolution and can introduce spectral leakage. Real NMR lines have a finite width set by $T_2^*$. We add it back by multiplying the FID by a window $e^{-t/T_2^{*}}$ before the Fourier transform. This is called a **line-broadening apodization**, which spectroscopists apply in processing to set the linewidth (trading resolution for signal-to-noise). (An optional cell in the Appendix of this module shows a **Trotterized** alternative, where the decay instead comes from real decoherence that grows with circuit depth.)\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"Sini-simulate natin ang ebolusyon ng molecule sa pamamagitan ng pag-map ng oras na $t$ sa mga *rotation angle*: ang mas malaking $t$ ay nangangahulugan ng mas malalaking $Z$ at $ZZ$ rotation, hindi ng mas mahaba o mas malalim na circuit. Kaya habang sumusulong nga ang simulated na state kasabay ng $t$ (iyan ang dahilan kung bakit nag-o-oscillate ang free induction decay (FID)), hindi mas matagal na naka-idle ang mga pisikal na qubit at walang decoherence na nakadepende sa $t$. Kaya ang simulated na FID ay hindi kailanman nabubulok nang kusa. Para sa walang-hanggang haba na ideyal na FID, walang-hanggang matalas ang mga spectral line; sa halip, ang may hangganan nating sampling window ay nagbibigay sa kanila ng may hangganang resolution at maaaring magdulot ng spectral leakage. Ang mga tunay na NMR line ay may hangganang lapad na itinatakda ng $T_2^*$. Idinaragdag natin ito pabalik sa pamamagitan ng pagpaparami sa FID ng window na $e^{-t/T_2^{*}}$ bago ang Fourier transform. Tinatawag itong **line-broadening apodization**, na ginagamit ng mga spectroscopist sa processing para itakda ang linewidth (ipinagpapalit ang resolution para sa signal-to-noise). (Ang isang opsyonal na cell sa Appendix ng module na ito ay nagpapakita ng **Trotterized** na alternatibo, kung saan ang decay ay nagmumula sa tunay na decoherence na lumalaki kasabay ng lalim ng circuit.)\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The AX system {#the-ax-system}"
msgstr "Ang AX system {#the-ax-system}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's start with the so-called AX system, a molecule consisting of two coupled, chemically distinct spins. The labels A and X denote the two chemically distinct spin environments. The letters are arbitrary, but the fact that they are far apart in the alphabet illustrates their chemical dissimilarity.\n"
msgstr "Magsimula tayo sa tinatawag na AX system, isang molecule na binubuo ng dalawang magkakakabit, chemically distinct na spin. Ang mga label na A at X ay tumutukoy sa dalawang chemically distinct na spin environment. Arbitraryo ang mga letra, pero ang katotohanang magkalayo sila sa alpabeto ay naglalarawan ng kanilang chemical na pagkakaiba.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Map and optimize {#map-and-optimize}"
msgstr "I-map at i-optimize {#map-and-optimize}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We write one function that, given the chemical-shift offsets $\\nu_i$ and couplings $J_{ij}$, returns a **parameterized** state-preparation circuit. The $\\pi/2$ pulse tips the spins to the equator, then the $Z$ rotations simulate the evolution of the state under the chemical-shift and $J$-coupling terms. Each $\\nu_i$ is a small precession *offset* in Hz relative to a chosen reference, or rotating-frame carrier. Here we set it manually; in a real molecule, electron shielding changes the local magnetic field and shifts each nucleus's resonance relative to that reference. This is modeled by the single-qubit $R_Z$ gates, one per spin, and each $\\nu_i$ fixes where that spin's peak sits in the spectrum. The two-qubit $ZZ$ gate applies the coupling $J_{ij}$, which splits each peak into a multiplet and sets the spacing between its lines.\n"
msgstr "Sumusulat tayo ng isang function na, kapag binigyan ng mga chemical-shift offset na $\\nu_i$ at mga coupling na $J_{ij}$, ay nagbabalik ng **parameterized** na state-preparation circuit. Ibinabagsak ng $\\pi/2$ pulse ang mga spin sa equator, tapos sini-simulate ng mga $Z$ rotation ang ebolusyon ng state sa ilalim ng mga chemical-shift at $J$-coupling na termino. Ang bawat $\\nu_i$ ay maliit na precession *offset* sa Hz kaugnay ng napiling reference, o rotating-frame carrier. Dito, mano-mano natin itong itinatakda; sa tunay na molecule, binabago ng electron shielding ang lokal na magnetic field at inililipat nito ang resonance ng bawat nucleus kaugnay ng reference na iyon. Imomodelo ito ng mga single-qubit na $R_Z$ gate, isa kada spin, at itinatakda ng bawat $\\nu_i$ kung saan nakaupo ang peak ng spin na iyon sa spectrum. Inilalapat ng two-qubit na $ZZ$ gate ang coupling na $J_{ij}$, na naghahati sa bawat peak tungo sa multiplet at nagtatakda ng pagitan ng mga linya nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Execute and post-process {#execute-and-post-process}"
msgstr "Isagawa at i-post-process {#execute-and-post-process}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We evaluate the circuit over a grid of evolution times, and the **Estimator** returns $\\langle X\\rangle$ and $\\langle Y\\rangle$ at each. We combine these into the complex FID $\\langle X\\rangle + i\\langle Y\\rangle$, apply the apodization window, and Fourier transform the result to obtain the spectrum.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuri natin ang circuit sa isang grid ng mga evolution time, at ibinabalik ng **Estimator** ang $\\langle X\\rangle$ at $\\langle Y\\rangle$ sa bawat isa. Pinagsasama natin ang mga ito sa complex FID na $\\langle X\\rangle + i\\langle Y\\rangle$, inilalapat ang apodization window, at kinukuha ang Fourier transform ng resulta para makuha ang spectrum.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/26bcc4bc-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/extracted-outputs/26bcc4bc-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Extension: An AX$_2$ system and the $n+1$ rule {#extension-an-ax-2-system-and-the-n1-rule}"
msgstr "Extension: Isang AX$_2$ system at ang $n+1$ rule {#extension-an-ax-2-system-and-the-n1-rule}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now add a second equivalent neighbor. In an **AX$_2$** system one **A** spin couples, with the same $J$, to two **equivalent X** spins. (Remember, X is just a label for the two equivalent spins; it has nothing to do with the Pauli $X$ operators. Every coupling below is still $ZZ$.) Spin A then sees four neighbor configurations $(\\uparrow\\uparrow,\\ \\uparrow\\downarrow,\\ \\downarrow\\uparrow,\\ \\downarrow\\downarrow)$; the two mixed ones coincide, so A is split into a **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2 \\Rightarrow n+1 = 3$ lines). Each X spin sees only A and stays a doublet. Equivalent spins do not split each other, so we include no X–X coupling.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon, magdagdag ng pangalawang equivalent na kapitbahay. Sa isang **AX$_2$** system, isang **A** na spin ang nagko-couple, sa parehong $J$, sa dalawang **equivalent na X** na spin. (Tandaan, label lang ang X para sa dalawang equivalent na spin; walang kinalaman ito sa mga Pauli $X$ operator. Ang bawat coupling sa ibaba ay $ZZ$ pa rin.) Nakikita ng spin A ang apat na configuration ng kapitbahay $(\\uparrow\\uparrow,\\ \\uparrow\\downarrow,\\ \\downarrow\\uparrow,\\ \\downarrow\\downarrow)$; nagtutugma ang dalawang halo, kaya nahahati ang A sa isang **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2 \\Rightarrow n+1 = 3$ linya). Ang bawat X spin ay A lang ang nakikita at nananatiling doublet. Hindi naghahati sa isa't isa ang mga equivalent na spin, kaya hindi tayo nagsasama ng X–X coupling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A real molecule with this pattern is **1,1,2-trichloroethane** (Cl$_2$CH–CH$_2$Cl). When targeting the hydrogen spins (which is the most common form of NMR), the single hydrogen proton in **CH** plays the role of **A**, and the two equivalent **CH$_2$** protons are the two **X** spins. Now just count neighbors: the CH proton has *two* neighbors (the CH$_2$ pair), so its signal splits into a **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2$); each CH$_2$ proton has only *one* neighbor (the CH), so the CH$_2$ signal is a **1:1 doublet** ($n=1$). Note that the group with *fewer* protons (CH) shows *more* lines; the line count reflects how many neighbors a signal has, not how many protons share it.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang tunay na molecule na may ganitong pattern ay ang **1,1,2-trichloroethane** (Cl$_2$CH–CH$_2$Cl). Kapag hydrogen spin ang tinutukan (ang pinakakaraniwang anyo ng NMR), ang nag-iisang hydrogen proton sa **CH** ang gumaganap bilang **A**, at ang dalawang equivalent na **CH$_2$** proton ang dalawang **X** na spin. Ngayon, bilangin lang ang mga kapitbahay: ang CH proton ay may *dalawang* kapitbahay (ang CH$_2$ pair), kaya nahahati ang signal nito sa **1:2:1 triplet** ($n=2$); ang bawat CH$_2$ proton ay may *isang* kapitbahay lang (ang CH), kaya ang CH$_2$ signal ay isang **1:1 doublet** ($n=1$). Pansinin na ang grupong may *mas kaunting* proton (CH) ay nagpapakita ng *mas maraming* linya; ang bilang ng linya ay sumasalamin kung ilang kapitbahay ang isang signal, hindi kung ilang proton ang nagbabahagi nito.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![1,1,2-trichloroethane: the CH proton is A, the two equivalent CH$_2$ protons are X](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/trichloroethane_ax.avif)\n"
msgstr "![1,1,2-trichloroethane: the CH proton is A, the two equivalent CH$_2$ protons are X](/learning/images/modules/quantum-mechanics/nmr-with-qiskit/trichloroethane_ax.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
